{{ macros.section(2, "M5 Macro & Templating Engine", id="m5") }}

`m5` is the string-templating macro engine at the bottom of `compose.mk`, essentially an m4[^m4] macro processor rebuilt in `make`.  Everything above it is written in its vocabulary: the [compiler]({{_}}/cmk/compiler) and its kind system, [banana block]({{_}}/cmk/banana) lowering, and the [hosted partition]({{_}}/cmk/compiler/#hosted-partition).

Two things make it worth learning even if you never touch the compiler.

**It is the ordinary way to write a macro.**  Vanilla `make` gives you raw positional reads that you must strip by hand, and that error out on a missing argument.  The `m5` accessors are strip-normalized, carry an optional form, and publish list operations on the argument itself:

```Makefile {.snippet .language-makefile}
# vanilla make
greet = hello, $(strip $(1))

# the same macro over m5: normalized arg-1, plus an optional arg-2
greet = hello, $(m5[1])$(if $(m5[2]?), and $(m5[2]?),)

# accessors work on the argument as a list
head = $(m5[1].first)
tail = $(m5[1].rest)
```

**Notation simulates a type system.**  A handful of sigils name what an operation *does*, so a glance at a name tells you its pole, and two classic `make` mistakes (reading a variable that was never set, evaluating text that was meant as data) become visible at the call site.

The `m5` section of {{macros.repo_link('compose.mk', name='compose.mk', github=github)}} is the authoritative catalog.  This page is the guided tour.

